{"c": null, "r": "I'm designing a Bank class that manages accounts with methods for opening accounts, depositing, withdrawing, checking balances, and transferring funds between accounts. The key requirements are validating that amounts are positive integers, preventing operations on non-existent accounts, ensuring transfers only happen between different accounts with sufficient funds, and raising appropriate errors for edge cases like duplicate accounts or negative initial balances.

I'm also noting that InsufficientFunds is a subclass of ValueError, which satisfies the test expectations, and that the validation order matters—for instance, checking if accounts exist before checking if a transfer is to the same account ensures KeyError is raised for unknown accounts rather than ValueError. Now I'm implementing the class with these constraints in mind. Checking the transfer logic for edge cases like unknown destination accounts and insufficient funds, then validating that amounts are positive integers. I'll create a module-level default bank instance and wrapper functions that delegate to it, making sure to preserve the legacy module-level functions while adding the new transfer function.
", "t": [["write_file", "{"path":"D:\\Projects\\local_ai\\strata-lab\\runs\\work\\hermes\\bank-1794053343\\bank.py","content":"\"\"\"Account helpers. Balances are integer cents.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass InsufficientFunds(ValueError):\n    \"\"\"Raised when a source balance is too small for a withdrawal or transfer.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass Bank:\n    \"\"\"A collection of named accounts; each Bank owns its own accounts.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self):\n        self._accounts = {}\n\n    def open_account(self, name, initial=0):\n        if initial < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"initial must be >= 0: \" + name)\n        if name in self._accounts:\n            raise ValueError(\"account exists: \" + name)\n        self._accounts[name] = initial\n\n    def deposit(self, name, amount):\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"amount must be > 0\")\n        self._require(name)\n        self._accounts[name] += amount\n\n    def withdraw(self, name, amount):\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"amount must be > 0\")\n        current = self._require(name)\n        if current < amount:\n            raise InsufficientFunds(\n                \"account %s has %s, need %s\" % (name, current, amount))\n        self._accounts[name] = current - amount\n\n    def balance(self, name):\n        return self._require(name)\n\n    def transfer(self, src, dst, amount):\n        if amount <= 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"amount must be > 0\")\n        if src == dst:\n            raise ValueError(\"cannot transfer to the same account: \" + src)\n        src_amount = self._require(src)\n        dst_amount = self._require(dst)\n        if src_amount < amount:\n            raise InsufficientFunds(\n                \"account %s has %s, need %s\" % (src, src_amount, amount))\n        self._accounts[src] = src_amount - amount\n        self._accounts[dst] = dst_amount + amount\n\n    def _require(self, name):\n        try:\n            return self._accounts[name]\n        except KeyError:\n            raise KeyError(\"unknown account: \" + name)\n\n\ndefault_bank = Bank()\n\n\ndef open_account(name, initial=0):\n    default_bank.open_account(name, initial)\n\n\ndef deposit(name, amount):\n    default_bank.deposit(name, amount)\n\n\ndef withdraw(name, amount):\n    default_bank.withdraw(name, amount)\n\n\ndef balance(name):\n    return default_bank.balance(name)\n\n\ndef transfer(src, dst, amount):\n    default_bank.transfer(src, dst, amount)\n"}"]]}

{"c": null, "r": "I need to add an `_equal` check to the else / list and dictionary branches. Current structure:

```
def _walk(a, b, path, ops):
    if isinstance(a, dict) and isinstance(b, dict):
        ... descend
    elif isinstance(a, list) and isinstance(b, list):
        ... descend
    else:
        ops.append("~ ...")
```

Currently, equal scalars end up falling into the else and producing `~`. We need to insert `elif _equal(a, b): return` before the else. Note: two equal dicts or two equal lists will descend and produce nothing — no problem. In the case of dict vs dict, it descends into the dict branch. In the case of dict vs list → else → `~`. This is correct per the spec.
", "t": [["patch", "{"new_string":"            else:\n                _walk(a[i], b[i], child, ops)\n    elif _equal(a, b):\n        return\n    else:\n        ops.append(\"~ %s: %s -> %s\" % (path, _fmt(a), _fmt(b)))","old_string":"            else:\n                _walk(a[i], b[i], child, ops)\n    else:\n        ops.append(\"~ %s: %s -> %s\" % (path, _fmt(a), _fmt(b)))","path":"D:\\Projects\\local_ai\\strata-lab\\runs\\work\\hermes\\jsondiff-1794053379\\jsondiff.py"}"]]}
